  "full_response": "Content Delivery Networks (CDNs) significantly improve web performance by optimizing the delivery of content to users. Here’s how they achieve this:\n\n### **1. Reduced Latency (Faster Load Times)**\n   - **Geographically Distributed Servers**: CDNs store cached copies of content (e.g., images, videos, scripts, HTML) in multiple **Points of Presence (PoPs)** worldwide. When a user requests content, the CDN serves it from the nearest server, reducing the distance data must travel.\n   - **Lower Round-Trip Time (RTT)**: Shorter physical distances between the user and the server mean faster data transmission, improving page load speeds.\n\n### **2. Load Balancing & Reduced Server Strain**\n   - **Traffic Distribution**: CDNs distribute requests across multiple servers, preventing any single origin server from becoming overwhelmed. This reduces the risk of crashes during traffic spikes (e.g., Black Friday sales, viral content).\n   - **Origin Shielding**: Some CDNs act as a buffer between the origin server and edge servers, reducing direct requests to the origin and improving efficiency.\n\n### **3. Caching & Static Content Optimization**\n   - **Edge Caching**: Frequently accessed content (e.g., images, CSS, JavaScript) is cached at edge servers, eliminating the need to fetch it from the origin server repeatedly.\n   - **Cache Invalidation & TTL (Time-to-Live)**: CDNs use smart caching policies to ensure users get fresh content while minimizing origin server requests.\n   - **Static vs. Dynamic Content**: While static content (unchanging files) is cached aggressively, dynamic content (e.g., personalized data) can be optimized via techniques like **edge computing** or **route optimization**.\n\n### **4. Improved Bandwidth Efficiency**\n   - **Compression & Minification**: CDNs often compress files (e.g., Gzip, Brotli) and minify CSS/JS to reduce file sizes, speeding up transfers.\n   - **HTTP/2 & HTTP/3 Support**: Modern CDNs leverage these protocols for multiplexing (parallel requests) and reduced latency (QUIC in HTTP/3).\n   - **TCP & TLS Optimization**: CDNs optimize connection handshakes (e.g., TLS 1.3) and use **Anycast routing** to reduce latency.\n\n### **5. DDoS Protection & Security Enhancements**\n   - **Traffic Scrubbing**: CDNs absorb and filter malicious traffic (e.g., DDo",
